1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,101
Here is where most teams lose the demo.

2
00:00:04,101 --> 00:00:09,226
The card cannot hold every model at once. So the obvious idea is, unload one, load the next.

3
00:00:09,226 --> 00:00:13,327
Do not do this. A cold load means starting a process,

4
00:00:13,327 --> 00:00:18,453
reading gigabytes off the disk, setting up the card, and warming everything up.

5
00:00:18,453 --> 00:00:24,604
Published measurements put that at 37 seconds for a small model, and 58 seconds for a vision model.

6
00:00:24,604 --> 00:00:28,192
Almost a minute, with a judge watching.

7
00:00:28,192 --> 00:00:32,293
The fix is to stop thinking about loading and unloading,

8
00:00:32,293 --> 00:00:35,368
and start thinking about where the weights live. Three tiers.

9
00:00:35,368 --> 00:00:39,469
Tier zero, pinned in VRAM. Never moves.

10
00:00:39,469 --> 00:00:44,595
The small helpers, the search models, the classifier. About two and a half gigabytes.

11
00:00:44,595 --> 00:00:47,670
Tier one, the model currently answering.

12
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:52,283
Tier two, the other models, held warm in ordinary computer memory.

13
00:00:52,283 --> 00:00:57,922
Waking one of those is just a copy, and it takes about one second instead of forty.

14
00:00:57,922 --> 00:01:03,560
And that is why system RAM, not just VRAM on the graphics card, is the specification that actually matters.
